    {
      "id": "p0053-b002",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Whenever something receives multiple translations, an interesting psychological phenomenon takes shape: people tend to unconsciously cling to the very first translation they encounter, and compare all other translations against it. I call this phenomenon \"imprinting\", based on a psychological term that refers to bow newborn animals naturally bond to the first things they learn to recognize.",
        "raw": "Whenever something receives multiple translations, an interesting\npsychological phenomenon takes shape: people tend to unconsciously\ncling to the very first translation they encounter, and compare all other\ntranslations against it. I call this phenomenon \"imprinting\", based on a\npsychological term that refers to bow newborn animals naturally bond to the\nfirst things they learn to recognize.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.09233179,
          0.09298571,
          0.68583653,
          0.19522857
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09029446,
          0.09227143,
          0.70517079,
          0.1963
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          2,
          6,
          9,
          10,
          11,
          14
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "只要同一作品有多个译版，就会出现一种有趣的心理现象：人们往往不自觉地依恋最先接触的译版，并拿它衡量其他版本。我借用心理学术语，将这种现象称为“印随”，这个词原指新生动物自然依附于最先学会辨认的对象。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "annotations": [],
      "layout": {
        "bbox": [
          0.0932384,
          0.09155714,
          0.70313346,
          0.1988
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "line_height": 1.45,
        "font_weight": 450,
        "background": "transparent",
        "text_align": "justify",
        "auto_height": true,
        "limit_bottom": 0.6464285714285715,
        "snap_to_guides": true,
        "resize_anchor": "left"
      },
      "calibration": {
        "bbox": [
          0.0932384,
          0.09155714,
          0.70313346,
          0.1988
        ],
        "font_size": 20,
        "leading": 29.0
      },
      "review_issues": []
    },
    {
      "id": "p0053-b003",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In translation, this imprinting phenomenon often leads to an excessive, irrational attachment to the first translation you encounter, regardless of the translation's quality or genuine flaws. Imprinting isn't limited to translation, of course, and can potentially occur with any sort of first experience.",
        "raw": "In translation, this imprinting phenomenon often leads to an excessive,\nirrational attachment to the first translation you encounter, regardless of the\ntranslation's quality or genuine flaws. Imprinting isn't limited to translation, of\ncourse, and can potentially occur with any sort of first experience.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.0932384,
          0.21211429,
          0.70081091,
          0.27825
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.09029446,
          0.2114,
          0.70517079,
          0.27932143
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          16,
          18,
          21,
          23
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "翻译中的印随现象，常让人不顾译文质量和实际缺点，过度、非理性地依恋最先见到的版本。当然，印随不局限于翻译，任何首次体验都有可能如此。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
      "annotations": [],
